#basePath P1_NativeLamprey
#include "Header.leaf"

:: Start
@exposed
@trigger    OnLocationChanged
// "NONE" ensures no other threads are in-progress
// global:currentPhase == 1 ensures player is in phase 1
@when       global:currentThread == "NONE", global:currentPhase == 1, location == "DNROffice"
@cutscene
// Lock all location on map
$call SetInThreadLocks()
$call DisableMapButton()
$call SetBackground("bg/bg-dnr-office.png")

// Mark start of thread
$set global:currentThread = "P1_NativeLamprey"

$call  ClearVisibleLines()
{@}     
       You figure the Department of Natural Resources must have something to start you with. This is their whole job, right?
       The office is nicely furnished with natural memorabilia, a straight-laced office man sitting behind the desk.

{@Paul}
       Mm, hello and welcome to the Department of Natural Resources. My name is Paul. How can I help you?

{@You}
       Um, hi Paul. I'm from Sea Grant and was wondering if you had any information about sea lamprey for me. 

{@}
       You show him the photo of the bite mark. 
       He stares at it for a moment, unblinking, like it's a tax return and not a fish with a hole in its side. 

{@Paul}
       Mm, yes, sea lamprey. They're not from around these parts. 

{@You}
       Really? Where'd they come from then?

{@Paul}
       Mm, the Atlantic Ocean. Hence the "sea" in sea lamprey. Mhm.
       But in the 1800s, the new canals that had been built allowed the sea lamprey to reach the Great Lakes.
       The Great Lakes are a perfect environment for sea lamprey: tributaries serve as great spawning locations and there's a variety of fish for them to eat. 
       The sea lamprey, together with unsusutainable fishing practices of the time, caused the population of other fish species to sharply decline. 

{@You}
       How come?
       
{@Paul}
       Mm. The fish in the Atlantic Ocean, which evolved alongside the sea lamprey, have defense mechanisms against sea lamprey.
       But fish in the Great Lakes don't, so they are more likely to die from sea lamprey bites or infections. 
       The issue isn't the sea lamprey as a species, but the fact that they were introduced into a new environment that was already struggling. //evidence chip here?
       
{@You}
       So why is this becoming a new issue now? I mean, the sea lamprey were introduced two hundred years ago. 

{@Paul}
       Hm. There are many techniques that have successfully been used to control the sea lamprey population.
       But climate change has put the ecosystem here under stress.
       Plus, during the pandemic, people couldn't go out to place traps and lampricide, which are two techniques used to control the population. 

{@You}
       So it's an issue of restarting those techniques that were used in the past to manage the population?
       Why not just try to, like, remove them all? Weren't things better in the past?

{@}
       He tilts his head at you, dark eyes set quizzicaly in a bored face. 

{@Paul}
       Are you from the area, Ms. Ana?

{@You}
       Uhhh. Yes? Though I've been away for a few years at college. 

{@Paul}
       Mhm. What's it been like being back?

{@You}
       I don't know. It's.. different. I guess. 
       It's not really what I remember. 
       I miss it. The way it was before, I mean. 

{@Paul}
       Mm. I've been here a long time. I've seen things come and go. 
       The future is always a gift. It is worth striving for. 

{@}
       You blink at him. He might have smiled, but it's hard to tell. 

{@Paul}
       I hope you will understand in the future. 
       But to answer your question, no, we are not trying to remove all the sea lamprey. 
       Not only is that not possible, but it's not how the environment works. Species come and go. 
       The point is to create ecological balance for all. 

{@}
       You think over his words. You think of the ice cream shop on your corner that closed your sophomore year. 
       You think about cool lake water lapping at your toes as a child. Of summers on boats and piers. 
       The photo of the bitten fish burns a hole in your pocket. 

{@You}
       I see. Thanks for your help, Paul. 

{@Paul}
       Mm. Of course. Come back any time. 

{@}
       Here's already back at his computer, professional-looking headphones over his ears. 

// TODO: Rest of thread here

$call GiveActionCard("example-action-card-1")

{@}
       This will be useful.

// TEMPORARY: skip to end
$goto .End


:: End
@exposed
@tracked
// Unlock next locations
$call UnlockLocation(ResearchLab)  // P1_Lamprey

// Mark end of thread
$set global:currentThread = "NONE"

// Go to Phase 1 Continue Hub
$goto P1_Hub.Continue